     "type": "read",
     "title": "My first American holiday season",
     "img": "fe:christmas_tree",
     "text": "Before I moved to Tampa, I thought I knew American holidays from movies. I was wrong. The movies don’t prepare you for the amount of food, decorations, and pumpkin spice.\n\nIt started on Halloween. I bought one small bag of candy, thinking a few kids might come. By 7:30 p.m., I had no candy left and a line of tiny princesses, dinosaurs and Spider-Men at my door. I gave away a box of French cookies that my mom had sent me. One little girl said, “These are weird. I love them.”\n\nThen came Thanksgiving at my in-laws’ house in Sarasota. My mother-in-law, Linda, is the kind of person who cooks for twenty even when there are eight people. There was a turkey, two kinds of stuffing, sweet potatoes with marshmallows on top (yes, really), and four pies. Before eating, everyone said what they were thankful for. When it was my turn, I said I was thankful for a family that made me feel at home in a new country. Linda cried. Then Tom’s uncle fell asleep watching football.\n\nIn December, the whole neighborhood lit up. Our neighbor Mike, the one who lives in the corner house, wrapped his palm trees in blue lights and put an inflatable Santa in sunglasses on his roof. At work, we did a secret Santa. I got a coffee mug that says “Ooh la la.” I think it was Marcus.\n\nBut my favorite was Gasparilla in January. Pirates, boats, music, and a million beads. I came home with a sunburn, thirty necklaces and a pirate hat. I think I’m officially a Tampa girl.",
